      "content": "Absolutely, I'd be glad to develop a deeper understanding. Here's an expanded take on Section 4 with academic frameworks embedded:\n\n---\n\nSection 4: Mechanisms of Systemic Racism – Academic Frameworks and Societal Mechanisms\n\n1. Developments in Institutional Theory (10 minutes)\n   - Introduce Institutional Theory to explain how norms and cognitive frameworks within institutions contribute to uniformity in thought, leading to systematic bias without conscious intention.\n   - For housing, this looks at how developers follow neighborhood trends based on established racial homogeneity.\n\n2. Critical Race Theory and LatCrit (15 minutes)\n   - Explore Critical Race Theory (CRT) and LatCrit, which provide an analytical framework for understanding how power structures perpetuate systemic racism.\n   - Discuss how the FHA’s policies institutionalized racism by adapting to secondary sources like biased mortgage lenders.\n\n3. Intersectionality (10 minutes)\n    - Discuss Kimberlé Crenshaw's concept of intersectionality to show how overlapping social identities, such as race and class, compound the experiences of discrimination in the housing and banking sectors.\n\n4. Case Studies and Group Discussion (20 minutes)\n   - Present case studies illustrating the interplay between policies, labor markets, residential segregation, and lending practices. Analyze each case",
