      "content": "It's important to encourage a multi-faceted understanding. Here's an approach to integrating different perspectives into the curriculum while still emphasizing the systemic nature of racism:\n\n---\n\nRevised Section 4: Broader Perspectives on Systemic Racism Mechanisms within Housing and Banking Sectors\n\n1. Systemic vs. Individual Perspectives (15 minutes)\n   - Acknowledge the role of individual agency and economic factors, but emphasize the disproportionate effect of societal norms and policies on minority communities.\n   - Discuss how systemic racism affects individual choices or creates barriers despite personal efforts.\n\n2. Behavioral Economics Approach (10 minutes)\n    - Introduce elements of behavioral economics that reveal decision-making biases and how this relates to housing and banking behaviors.\n\n3. Macroeconomic Factors (15 minutes)\n    - Delve into how broader economic trends and policies can disproportionately affect racial minority groups, helping to contextualize systemic racism within economic movements and shifts.\n\n4. Case Studies with a Twist (20 minutes)\n    - Re-examine previously discussed case studies to highlight both individual and systemic factors, analyzing the interplay between personal agency, institutional structures, and overarching economic trends. Encourage students to consider how each factor contributes to the perpetuation of systemic racism.\n\nBy presenting these frameworks alongside CRT and intersectionality",
